 - or a generation before that. $100k is probably what a graduate makes these days in the stem fields right out of school. So, the above dataset would be skewed both sides by folks at either extreme of my example. Folks from two generations ago (who entered the job market 40 years ago, let's say) would have taken way longer to get to $100k than folks who entered the job market three years ago (who ought to have $100k net worth today, if they graduated with no debt)!\nA better question would be - at what age did you first have a net worth that was x times your annual income?\n\nan_asker\n    Posts: 5512\n    Joined: Thu Jun 27, 2013 2:15 pm\n\nThis is just me, not including my wife (who came in with some assets of her own.) From this example alone, it does appear the first 100k was the hardest.\nCombination of: good paying job, refi educational loans at low rates, wife who shares similar values, frugal living (I take busses instead of trains to go into the city), mega backdoor Roth, maxing out 529 and other tax shelters, riding the bull market, paying for vacations with hotel points and airline miles, etc. etc.\n\nImage: User avatar\n\nlegio XX\n    Posts: 750\n    Joined: Tue Jul 15, 2008 6:37 am\n    Location: Stack 12\n\n### Re: What age did you reach 100K networth?\n\nPost by legio XX » Sun Jan 12, 2020 8:08 am\n\n> carofe wrote: Thu May 19, 2016 11:03 am\n> \n> . . . I collected 110 response, and I got this results:\n> . . . 95% Perc. 39.00 (95% of the Boglehead users had at least 100k net worth at age 39)\n\nWow! Sorry to skew the stats, but my net worth was probably negative until sometime in my 50s when IRA surpassed outstanding debt. Hit 100 k around age 60 thanks to unexpected inheritance. Current status, no debt and halfway to two commas. Don't expect to reach them and not losing any sleep over that. It's a weird world when a guy in his 30s can cry poor because he isn't a millionaire.\n\njibantik\n    Posts: 568\n    Joined: Fri Nov 24, 2017 12:05 pm\n\n##